// Multi-visit D22: ONE shared status for both purposes. EN_ROUTE / ON_SITE / IN_PROGRESS are
// added here because they are properties of a TRIP, not of a job - they retire from JobStatus in
// slice S4, where Job.status becomes derived. S1 only ever writes SCHEDULED / COMPLETED /
// CANCELLED, matching what the lead side does today.
//
// REQUESTED is RETIRED (D22a). It encoded "this lead needs a visit booked", which is not a state
// of a visit at all - it is the ABSENCE of one. Keeping it forced a placeholder row to be minted
// with every lead and re-minted after every cancellation, and it cannot survive multi-visit: with
// several live visits per lead there is no single row for the placeholder to be. The three
// needs-scheduling queries flip from "has a REQUESTED visit" to "has no live visit".
enum VisitStatus {
  SCHEDULED
  EN_ROUTE
  ON_SITE
  IN_PROGRESS
  COMPLETED
  CANCELLED
}

// D3: a visit is a visit; only the parent and the purpose differ. This is what retires the word
// "walkthrough" from the platform (closing SRVW-57) - a lead's visit is one whose purpose is
// WALKTHROUGH, a job's is one whose purpose is WORK.
enum VisitPurpose {
  WORK
  WALKTHROUGH
}

